  <RESPONSE>
    Yes, completely independent. Here's the breakdown:
    
    **Your passwords are separate things:**
    - **LUKS password** — set at disk encryption setup, used at boot only
    - **KDE lock screen password** — your Linux user account password (via PAM)
    - **sudo password** — also your Linux user account password (via PAM)
    
    The lock screen and sudo share the same underlying password (your user account login), but **disabling the lock screen doesn't touch that password at all** — sudo will still work exactly as before.
    
    ---
    
    **To disable the lock screen password in KDE Plasma 6:**
    
    System Settings → **Screen Locking** → uncheck **"Lock screen automatically after X minutes"**
    
    That's it. The screen will still go blank after idle via normal power management (DPMS), but waking it won't require a password. Sudo is completely unaffected.
    
    **Trade-off to be aware of:** With automatic locking off, your session is unprotected if you walk away. You can still manually lock with **Meta+L** when you want security (e.g., leaving your desk). Since you have LUKS, you're already protected against physical disk theft — the main risk is just someone sitting down at an unlocked, running session.
    
    For the slideshow use case, the practical setup would be:
    - Lock screen automatic locking: **off**
    - Desktop wallpaper: set to your Jellyfin plugin → always running, no password to dismiss
    - Manual lock (Meta+L) when needed
  </RESPONSE>

